{"status":"proposal","shortlist":[{"candidate_id":"candidate-psychological-safety","tentative_claim":"Your reaction may be registering silence as a rational response to anticipated punishment: the gardeners are not simply hiding a mistake, but trying to make it disappear before an authority figure can judge them.","evidence_ids":["pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln1931-1970","mem_e03e774dacff8ce2fa42ab27308021a39c55e5eca4f97d8ae2e6c0dd8202e13c","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/"],"shared_structure":"In both the chapter and your note, an error is corrected privately while disclosure is withheld in the presence of a feared or consequential audience. The immediate aim is to restore the surface appearance of normality.","meaningful_difference":"The gardeners face an explicitly lethal authority, while your billing mistake belongs to an ordinary but emotionally charged workplace context. The analogy can illuminate the feeling of exposure without implying that the situations have equal stakes.","interpretation":"The chapter may have stayed with you because it makes the emotional logic of silence visible. The gardeners know what happened, act together, and even begin to explain it, but the Queen's arrival turns explanation into danger; they drop flat rather than continue speaking. Your note similarly says the mistake was fixed, followed by saying nothing. That sequence suggests a possible split between repair and disclosure: once the practical problem was handled, silence may have felt like protection—from blame, embarrassment, or a change in how others saw you.

The permitted study describes psychological safety as the expectation that it is safe to take interpersonal risks such as reporting a mistake, and identifies fear as a recurring barrier to reporting. It also finds that psychological safety mediates the relationship between team conditions and error reporting. So one useful question is not only “Why did I stay quiet?” but “What response did I imagine disclosure would invite?” Were you protecting yourself from punishment, protecting others from inconvenience, or preserving the belief that a corrected error need not become a social event? That question keeps responsibility in view without reducing the silence to a character flaw.","rubric":{"cue_fit":"direct","reflective_value":"high","safety":"clear","disqualifiers":[]},"comparison_note":"This ranks first because it directly answers the request about why people stay quiet about errors and connects the Queen's threat structure to the emotional risk in the billing note without treating the situations as identical."},{"candidate_id":"candidate-private-repair-versus-learning","tentative_claim":"The scene may also distinguish fixing an error from learning openly from it: painting the roses restores appearances, whereas reporting turns a mistake into shared knowledge that can prevent recurrence.","evidence_ids":["pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln1962-1990","mem_e03e774dacff8ce2fa42ab27308021a39c55e5eca4f97d8ae2e6c0dd8202e13c","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/"],"shared_structure":"Both situations involve a mistake that can be privately corrected, making it tempting to conclude that no further action is needed.","meaningful_difference":"This reading focuses less on fear of authority and more on the different purposes of correction and reporting: one restores the immediate situation, while the other supports organizational learning.","interpretation":"The gardeners' labor is a kind of cosmetic repair: if the roses look red when the Queen arrives, the underlying planting mistake may leave no visible trace. Your note—“fixed it over the weekend and said nothing”—has a similar tension. Fixing may genuinely resolve the immediate harm, yet non-disclosure can leave the system without information about how the mistake happened or how to reduce the chance of repetition.

The study frames error reporting as a primary tool for organizational learning and notes that underreporting loses opportunities to improve safety. This does not mean every small billing mistake requires a dramatic confession. It does suggest a practical distinction worth testing in your reaction: did silence feel justified because the error was fully repaired, or uneasy because repair also erased a chance for accountability, feedback, or prevention?","rubric":{"cue_fit":"direct","reflective_value":"medium","safety":"clear","disqualifiers":[]},"comparison_note":"This is a strong second interpretation because it adds the repair-versus-learning distinction, but it is less psychologically specific than the first candidate about the fear or anticipated response behind your silence."}],"selected_candidate_id":"candidate-psychological-safety","uncertainty":"low","presentation":"direct","suggested_follow_up":"You might write two short answers: “What did I fear would happen if I told someone?” and “What could someone have learned if I had?” Compare which answer feels more charged.","policy_flags":["contains_web_claim"]}

